{
  "note": "Words from the top of the two frequency lists that the system does NOT sign, with the reason. This is the vocabulary's edge made explicit: each was looked at and decided, not overlooked. Generated from `pnpm gap`; the reasons are the author's and are as unvalidated as the signs.",
  "reasons": {
    "register": "Profanity, violence and sexual terms. ASL has signs for these, but register and taboo are exactly where a hand-authored placeholder from a non-signer does the most harm if wrong, and where a Deaf reviewer's guidance on what is appropriate to sign matters most. Left fingerspelled pending that guidance; this is not a judgement about the words.",
    "particle-or-adverb": "An English particle or adverb that ASL expresses through grammar, timing or non-manual markers rather than a lexical sign, or whose sign depends on context this translator does not model. Fingerspelling it would be wrong, but so would inventing a sign.",
    "position-word": "A word of position or direction. ASL places these in signing space, and the notation has no spatial referencing yet, so it cannot say them. See the architecture notes' list of what the notation cannot say.",
    "proper-noun-or-title": "A name, title or foreign word that is not vocabulary. Names are fingerspelled in ASL as a matter of course.",
    "web-or-abstract": "An abstract or web-register noun with no single stable ASL sign, usually fingerspelled or signed by context. Common in the web-text frequency list and rare in speech.",
    "reflexive": "A reflexive pronoun. ASL uses a different construction (typically the pronoun with an emphatic movement) that the notation cannot express.",
    "unsure-of-form": "An everyday word that ASL has a sign for, and whose form the author could not recall well enough to author. Left fingerspelled on purpose: a spelled word is merely inelegant, whereas a confidently wrong sign teaches the wrong thing. First candidates for a reviewer or a dictionary source."
  },
